Yes. You can lose the word and still remember the sound, the look on someone’s face, or the exact moment the room changed. Give that memory a new title and you can change which part comes forward when you tell it.

Let’s do it with the mechanic. Forget vellinch for a second. Here’s the scene again.

The mechanic shut off the engine. We stood there without talking. A tiny click came from under the hood. He nodded before I’d even registered it.

I’d call that The Second Silence if I wanted to name the experience of hearing something only after you think the room has gone quiet. I’d call it What He Was Waiting For if the thing that stuck with me was the mechanic’s ear for a sound I would’ve missed. Same click. Different memory to tell.

And now the second silence can travel. The song ends, the crowd takes a breath, and in that little gap you hear somebody in the back still singing. You can carry the experience into a whole new scene, even if vellinch never comes back to you.